### 4. DEVICE AND BROWSER CAPABILITY DETECTION

Implement reliable feature detection before offering to start an immersive AR session.

The system must determine, where the browser exposes the necessary APIs:

- Whether the current page is in a secure context.
- Whether the relevant XR API exists.
- Whether an `immersive-ar` session is supported.
- Whether the required rendering and tracking features are available.
- Whether a suitable hit-test capability exists, if required by the chosen placement workflow.
- Whether the required permission-policy configuration allows the operation.
- Whether the current rendering device and graphics configuration can initialize the experience.
- Whether the user has denied a required permission.
- Whether the session has ended, lost tracking, or failed during startup.

Use genuine feature detection and capability checks rather than user-agent strings alone.

For a WebXR implementation, investigate the actual supported Three.js XR APIs and their compatibility with the repository's installed version.

Official technical references:
- WebXR Device API: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebXR_Device_API
- WebXR permissions and security: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebXR_Device_API/Permissions_and_security
- WebXR lifecycle: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebXR_Device_API/Lifecycle
- Three.js WebXRManager: https://threejs.org/docs/pages/WebXRManager

Requirements:

1. Do not assume all browsers support `immersive-ar`.
2. Do not assume that desktop browsers provide handheld-camera AR.
3. Do not assume that a browser supports hit testing merely because basic WebXR functionality exists.
4. Request an AR session only after an explicit user action.
5. Handle unsupported features without uncaught exceptions.
6. Display a clear explanation when the current device/browser cannot run the immersive experience.
7. Avoid using an inaccurate UA-based browser detector as the only compatibility check.
8. Distinguish an unsupported browser from a permission denial or runtime failure.

If a required feature is unavailable, gracefully disable the relevant AR controls and provide the supported non-AR visualization fallback.

### 5. AR EXPERIENCE MODES AND HONEST FALLBACKS

Provide clearly named viewing modes.

**Mode A — Immersive AR**

Use the actual supported immersive-AR session and its real tracking capabilities.

Where available, use surface hit testing or another verified AR placement mechanism to help the user choose a physical placement point.

**Mode B — Standard 3D Site Preview**

Use the existing Plotify 3D scene for users or devices that cannot run immersive AR.

This mode may provide orbit, zoom, pan, model inspection, and a site-context view if the existing viewer supports them.

It must not claim to show the model physically anchored to the real surroundings.

**Mode C — Manually Calibrated Placement Preview**

Offer manual placement or orientation controls when they can be implemented accurately enough for a useful preview.

This mode must disclose that the alignment is user-calibrated and does not establish surveyed geographic placement.

Do not display a live camera background as though it were tracking-aware AR when the model is merely superimposed on camera imagery without reliable spatial tracking.

Do not pretend that a screenshot, camera permission, or device gyroscope alone provides accurate plane detection, world anchoring, or geographic registration.

If marker-based placement is considered, implement it only if a genuine supported detection method exists and its marker requirements, tracking behaviour, and scale assumptions are documented. Do not invent a marker detector or claim marker tracking merely because a marker image can be uploaded.

